I do price-id and mark price of every item category I found (well,  
potions, scrolls, rings, wands, spellbooks).

I am almost never able to exactly identify an item by price-iding and  
elimination, but it also helps in selecting which items to identify first  
and which (not) to try.

More specifically :
50$ potions are (almost) useless => holy water feeder, #dip for sickness.
250$ potions are oil/acid => easy id.
300$ potions contains gain level => very important in Slash'em. Plus gain  
ability, always good.

20$, 50$ and 60$ scrolls are a free id.
80$ scrolls are either enchant armor or remove curse, very useful too.
200$ scrolls contains amnesia => one of the first things I spend my id on.
300$ scrolls are too valuable to be read-ided, other OK (if precaution  
taken, of course).

500$ wands are good news and the one I do try in shop...
175$ wands are also nice since 1/2 chance of permanent engraving tool...
But anyway, wands are easilly ided.

200$ rings contains SD and FA, two very usefull rings, often an early  
identify too.
300$ rings contains powerful but dangerous rings (conflict and polymorph).  
Don't put them on anywhere... But now you already know that :-)

price-iding spellbooks is nice to know if you've got nice chances to read  
it or not (in vanilla, not in [S]). I often read book with, say, more than  
70% chances of success. Especially if it's for potentially nice spells.


That's a bit tedious and somewhat lengthly (you'll have to walk in every  
space of the shop, pick up object (rename it) and drop it again. But it's  
often worth teh effort.
(of course, I won't do that if I do not have time, typically being a  
slash'em vampire where food is so important in the early game)

